1
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:03,923
In this video,
we're going to talk about poll and select.

2
00:00:03,923 --> 00:00:08,320
So understand the use of poll and
select in kernel drivers.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:13,002
Understand how to do seeking in your
kernel driver and associate read,

4
00:00:13,002 --> 00:00:17,851
write open function implementation
controlling access to your driver.

5
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:23,877
So poll and select can be used to
determine whether a device can be opened

6
00:00:23,877 --> 00:00:28,471
without blocking or whether you need to
wait for the descriptor to become ready.

7
00:00:28,471 --> 00:00:33,476
So these are implementing the poll
function that you'd use from user

8
00:00:33,476 --> 00:00:38,320
space to figure out is there data
available on my driver endpoint.

9
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:41,749
Is my reader right command going to block?

10
00:00:41,749 --> 00:00:45,811
And it's supported by a poll method in
the device driver which looks like this.

11
00:00:45,811 --> 00:00:50,588
That takes a poll table structure that's
provided to you by the kernel and

12
00:00:50,588 --> 00:00:55,443
a file pointer that references the
particular open command that was used to

13
00:00:55,443 --> 00:00:57,151
open the file operation.

14
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:02,732
So looking at an example of
poll implementation in scull,

15
00:01:02,732 --> 00:01:07,210
we can see that the kernel really
does a lot of the heavy lifting for

16
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:12,343
us there poll weight functions here
that do whatever needs to get done based

17
00:01:12,343 --> 00:01:17,069
on these wait queues that we've
talked about in previous lectures or

18
00:01:17,069 --> 00:01:22,240
in previous videos where we talked
about how to wait on device operations.

19
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:26,857
So you're just going to call
poll_wait with inq and outqs and

20
00:01:26,857 --> 00:01:31,671
pass in the poll_table that is
passed to you from the kernel.

21
00:01:31,671 --> 00:01:36,732
So this is going to take care of
the details of what the kernels are,

22
00:01:36,732 --> 00:01:42,545
what the user's actually waiting for
and just fill in based on the current

23
00:01:42,545 --> 00:01:48,943
state of your waitq that you've set up for
waiting on data and input or output qs.

24
00:01:48,943 --> 00:01:54,843
So then after calling poll_wait
the bit mask that you return from poll

25
00:01:54,843 --> 00:02:00,443
needs to be set based on some rules
about the availability of read or

26
00:02:00,443 --> 00:02:05,543
write on your driver and
we'll talk about those in a minute,

27
00:02:05,543 --> 00:02:09,743
but again these wait qs
are just going to be defined,

28
00:02:09,743 --> 00:02:13,443
they're the same ones that we're using for

29
00:02:13,443 --> 00:02:18,464
waiting on read or
write availability inside your driver.

30
00:02:18,464 --> 00:02:23,021
So the rules that you need to use to
handle both the read and the poll or read

31
00:02:23,021 --> 00:02:28,179
write and poll implementations when there
is data available in the input buffer,

32
00:02:28,179 --> 00:02:32,920
so when you're asking for data from
the device and that data is available.

33
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:36,100
The read should always return
immediately with at least 1 byte.

34
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:39,531
So you don't need to return
the full length of the request but

35
00:02:39,531 --> 00:02:42,780
you need to return something
if there is data available.

36
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:46,340
This is kind of like the rule that you
need when you implement your read handler.

37
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:51,178
And also poll should return poll in and
read normally in other words there's

38
00:02:51,178 --> 00:02:55,504
data available that and
you're signaling that back to user space.

39
00:02:55,504 --> 00:03:00,487
When there's no data in the buffer,
the read should either block

40
00:03:00,487 --> 00:03:05,016
until at least 1 byte is there
if you're in blocking mode or

41
00:03:05,016 --> 00:03:09,638
if you specify non blocking mode
it should return -EAGAIN to

42
00:03:09,638 --> 00:03:14,620
say that you need to poll later for
data available and the read q and

43
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:18,470
then poll should report
unreadable in this case.

44
00:03:18,470 --> 00:03:22,651
And so the read flags that you return
in that mask value should all be 0.

45
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:30,548
And at the end of file, the read should
return immediately with a value of 0.

46
00:03:30,548 --> 00:03:37,741
And poll should report this POLLHUP which
means there's no data left in the buffer.

47
00:03:37,741 --> 00:03:42,152
So there's a distinguish here
between there's no data left in

48
00:03:42,152 --> 00:03:46,733
the buffer because there's nothing
written to the device yet but

49
00:03:46,733 --> 00:03:49,741
there's something that will be written.

50
00:03:49,741 --> 00:03:54,039
So this is all based on the design of your
device driver as opposed to there's not

51
00:03:54,039 --> 00:03:58,017
going to be anything else in the buffer
because you're at the end of file,

52
00:03:58,017 --> 00:04:01,418
this is all based on how you've
designed your device driver and

53
00:04:01,418 --> 00:04:04,151
what you're trying to
signal back to user space.

54
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:10,612
So, similarly to read we have rules
on write on how we should handle

55
00:04:10,612 --> 00:04:15,919
the write when there's either space
available or no space available and

56
00:04:15,919 --> 00:04:21,250
how poll should indicate what the current
status of the write buffer is.

57
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:24,233
So when there is space available
in the output buffer and

58
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:28,293
you have a way that you can handle write,
write should always return without

59
00:04:28,293 --> 00:04:32,311
delay accepting at least 1 byte and
writing at least 1 byte in the buffer.

60
00:04:32,311 --> 00:04:36,040
Again similar to read if you've been
asked to write a large amount of data,

61
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:38,310
you don't necessarily
have to write it all.

62
00:04:38,310 --> 00:04:43,073
But you should write at least 1 byte
if you have the ability to write 1

63
00:04:43,073 --> 00:04:45,221
byte into your device buffer.

64
00:04:45,221 --> 00:04:50,136
And then poll should report POLLOUT or
[INAUDIBLE] Normally

65
00:04:50,136 --> 00:04:54,960
in other words you would not
block on a write in this case.

66
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:59,395
But conversely when there is no space in
the output buffer, so the buffer is full,

67
00:04:59,395 --> 00:05:03,831
then a write needs to block until space is
freed if you're in blocking mode or if you

68
00:05:03,831 --> 00:05:08,719
specified O_NONBLOCK do the same thing, it
doesn't read and return -EAGAIN saying you

69
00:05:08,719 --> 00:05:12,880
need to keep calling this waiting for
space available in the output buffer.

70
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:16,972
And similarly poll should
report that the file is not

71
00:05:16,972 --> 00:05:20,700
writable by setting
the return flags all to 0.

72
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:26,473
So one thing that might be a little bit
confusing about write is that it mentioned

73
00:05:26,473 --> 00:05:32,075
before that as long as there's data
available room available in the buffer,

74
00:05:32,075 --> 00:05:35,607
you want to write at least
1 byte immediately and

75
00:05:35,607 --> 00:05:40,516
you might think that if O_NONBLOCK
is not set and we want to block and

76
00:05:40,516 --> 00:05:45,516
there's some type of operation that
needs to happen to transfer data

77
00:05:45,516 --> 00:05:49,565
from a buffer to the device
that we might want to buffer or

78
00:05:49,565 --> 00:05:54,171
we might want to block for
that actual data transfer to happen.

79
00:05:54,171 --> 00:05:58,680
In other words so we might want to wait
until the data is written all the way

80
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:01,840
from the buffer directly into the device.

81
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:07,577
But it turns out that's not really the way
that write should be handled in the Linux

82
00:06:07,577 --> 00:06:12,817
kernel because we want to optimize for
the case where we buffer by default and

83
00:06:12,817 --> 00:06:16,726
so that transfer from the right
buffer into the device,

84
00:06:16,726 --> 00:06:19,840
most likely takes a lot
of time to complete.

85
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:22,775
And we don't we don't
want to have to make users or

86
00:06:22,775 --> 00:06:25,870
user space applications wait for
that by default.

87
00:06:25,870 --> 00:06:29,965
So regardless of the setting of blocker or
nonblock,

88
00:06:29,965 --> 00:06:34,242
you don't want to typically
make a device driver wait for

89
00:06:34,242 --> 00:06:38,740
transfer to complete all
the way out to the device.

90
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:42,884
And the reason is we've already talked
about the fsync call that you can make

91
00:06:42,884 --> 00:06:46,261
from user space and so
you can implement fsync in your driver.

92
00:06:46,261 --> 00:06:50,214
And if the user space application cares
whether the data has been written all

93
00:06:50,214 --> 00:06:54,350
the way out to the device, they can use
that fsync call to determine whether that

94
00:06:54,350 --> 00:06:56,161
is or is not the case or to force it.

95
00:06:59,051 --> 00:07:02,300
So in addition to handling read and write,

96
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:08,370
you can think of the case where we want to
seek to a particular position in a file.

97
00:07:08,370 --> 00:07:10,072
So this is the seek commands and

98
00:07:10,072 --> 00:07:13,480
the llseek command that we've
talked about from user space.

99
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:17,673
And in that case the default case here
is just that we use that file pointer

100
00:07:17,673 --> 00:07:21,661
that we're passing as the first
argument to read or write and just set

101
00:07:21,661 --> 00:07:25,820
a position in that that determines
where we want to seek to in the device.

102
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:30,883
So the default implementation that
the kernel provides simply just

103
00:07:30,883 --> 00:07:36,330
sets that value and then read and
write can operate on that as necessary.

104
00:07:36,330 --> 00:07:41,444
But if you have some type of custom
operation that you need to do with seek,

105
00:07:41,444 --> 00:07:46,910
you can implement your own llseek file
operation in the file operation table.

106
00:07:46,910 --> 00:07:50,567
And you can also say that for
my particular device it doesn't make

107
00:07:50,567 --> 00:07:53,958
sense to try to seek to different
locations and so you can just

108
00:07:53,958 --> 00:07:58,450
call nonseekable_open which would prevent
seek from being supported at all.

109
00:07:58,450 --> 00:08:03,139
And that often make sense if you
are working with data flow as opposed to

110
00:08:03,139 --> 00:08:05,771
mapping to some kind of memory buffer.

111
00:08:05,771 --> 00:08:09,919
And the kernel gives examples of serial
port and keyboard where it doesn't really

112
00:08:09,919 --> 00:08:13,885
make sense to try to seek to a particular
location in the serial port because you're

113
00:08:13,885 --> 00:08:18,212
just processing characters as they come in
one after another and same for a keyboard,

114
00:08:18,212 --> 00:08:20,830
your processing keystrokes,
one after another.

115
00:08:20,830 --> 00:08:24,190
It doesn't really make sense to
seek back to old keystrokes.

116
00:08:24,190 --> 00:08:28,612
And so for those type of devices you
just call nonseekable_open in your open

117
00:08:28,612 --> 00:08:32,066
function and that would get rid
of this default behavior and

118
00:08:32,066 --> 00:08:35,961
just return a failure if there
are attempts to seek on your driver.

119
00:08:37,039 --> 00:08:39,939
Another thing mentioned
in chapter six has to do

120
00:08:39,939 --> 00:08:43,000
with the access control
that you can do from open.

121
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,110
So, there's a couple of different
examples that are provided in the next

122
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:51,419
device driver example code that show how
you can control access to your device

123
00:08:51,419 --> 00:08:53,040
through the open command.

124
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:58,003
So they give a single open example
where there's only one process that can

125
00:08:58,003 --> 00:09:02,804
open at a time and so there's atomic
value that you obtain in the open and

126
00:09:02,804 --> 00:09:04,830
you release that in release.

127
00:09:04,830 --> 00:09:11,480
So you can block in the open command
based on any requirements of your driver.

128
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:16,087
There's also a single user one that
compares the process uID to determine

129
00:09:16,087 --> 00:09:17,573
whether there's one or

130
00:09:17,573 --> 00:09:21,744
more users using the driver and
you can return -EBUSY when in use.

131
00:09:21,744 --> 00:09:26,042
So really the book is just showing you
that you have a couple different ways to

132
00:09:26,042 --> 00:09:30,471
control access to your driver and you can
do that in open and you can do the kind of

133
00:09:30,471 --> 00:09:34,400
blocking or non blocking that we've
talked about and read or write.

134
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:38,337
You can also do that and just prevent
access in open if that's desirable for

135
00:09:38,337 --> 00:09:39,726
your particular driver.